  "id": "Compound::DB00248 - Gene::147",
  "verbalization": "Cabergoline, a member of the ergoline class of compounds, exhibits a strong mechanistic link to Adra1b through its interaction with the dopamine agonist class. This is supported by multiple paths indicating that Cabergoline binds to Adra1b via a common ancestor in the nitrogen molecular entity, which is also associated with dopamine agonists. The evidence suggests that Cabergoline's interaction with Adra1b is mediated through a shared biological pathway, likely involving dopamine receptor modulation. The presence of multiple paths with overlapping lowest common ancestors reinforces the notion of a conserved mechanism across different drug–target pairs.\n\nThe evidence also highlights that Cabergoline is associated with adverse effects such as pericarditis constrictive and excessive daytime sleepiness, which are both linked to Bromocriptine, a closely related ergoline compound that binds to Adra1b. These findings suggest that the therapeutic effects and side effects of Cabergoline may be influenced by its interaction with Adra1b, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of considering the broader biological context when evaluating the relationship between Cabergoline and Adra1b.",
